findings.body:
**A failed run of this workflow labels the PR `pr/ci-failing` from then on.** ([pr-status.yml#L80-L92](https://github.com/AlexisBalayre/pupitre/blob/89055ad1bb046cc64731fa7f8c61a36de2eaeba5/.github/workflows/pr-status.yml#L80-L92))

`listForRef` on the PR head SHA also returns this workflow's own `status` check run. When a run fails, the failed check stays on the head SHA. Every later recompute (push to main, `pull_request_target`) matches it with `bad.has(c.conclusion)` and labels the PR `pr/ci-failing`. Under the default `filter=latest`, the failed check is replaced only by a later `status` run *on the same SHA*. Only `reopened`, `ready_for_review` and `converted_to_draft` produce one.

On fork PRs this happens on every review. `pull_request_review` from a fork runs with a read-only `GITHUB_TOKEN`, whatever `permissions:` says. The first `createLabel` gets a 403, not a 422, and this guard rethrows it:

```js
} catch (e) {
  if (e.status !== 422) throw e;
}
```

So the job fails red on each review. The next push to main then labels the PR `pr/ci-failing` until a new commit lands, and the next review does it again. Same-repo PRs get poisoned the same way by a `removeLabel` 404 when two runs overlap, or by any transient API error.

Suggested fix:
- Leave this workflow's own check runs out of the count.
- Skip `pull_request_review` on fork PRs: a job `if:` such as `github.event_name != 'pull_request_review' || github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository`, or an early return in the script. Fork PRs are still relabelled by `pull_request_target` and push-to-main.
